    {
      "paperId": "/article/10.1007/s12524-025-02219-7",
      "doi": "10.1007/s12524-025-02219-7",
      "title": "A Note on Exploring the Future of Earth Observation Sensors: Technological Advancements, Strategic Management and Geopolitical Implications",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Mudit Mathur, Manjusree Panchagnula, Prakash Chauhan"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "Space exploration and satellite-based systems are undergoing a profound transformation, driven by rapid advancements in spacecraft and sensor technologies. The integration of artificial intelligence (AI), quantum communication, and modular spacecraft design is reshaping the capabilities of space missions, enabling autonomous decision-making, precise sensing, and greater operational flexibility. In parallel, hyperspectral imaging, high resolution multi-spectral imaging, quantum-enhanced sensors, and sustainable in-orbit edge computing are expanding the range and precision of data collection. These applications are crucial for environmental monitoring, military surveillance and resource exploration. However, to sustain long-term growth, strategic constraints like orbital congestion, space debris, regulatory uncertainty, and resource limitations must be managed effectively. Moreover, geopolitical dynamics play a critical role in shaping the future of space power for strategic use. The democratization of space technologies, with the rise of regional space powers alongside traditional superpowers, has introduced new layers of complexity in the governance of space assets. In this paper, the principal drivers of disruption, technological advancements, the global scenario of leading space agencies, future navigation of space sensors and the geopolitical competition in the space sector are discussed and strategic points are recommended to the stake holders and defense organizations.",
      "abstract_not_available": false,
      "snippet": "Space exploration and satellite-based systems are undergoing a profound transformation, driven by rapid advancements in spacecraft and sensor...",
