    {
      "paperId": "/chapter/10.1007/978-3-032-26828-0_1",
      "doi": "10.1007/978-3-032-26828-0_1",
      "title": "Remote Sensing in Coastal Ecosystems: Principles and Applications",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Kousik Das Malakar"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "This chapter explores the role of remote sensing in advancing the study and management of coastal ecosystems. Coastal regions are highly dynamic and often difficult to monitor through conventional field methods alone. In this context, remote sensing provides a practical and efficient means of observing large areas over time. The chapter outlines the fundamental principles that guide its application, including sensor characteristics, spatial and temporal resolution, and commonly used data acquisition approaches. These foundational concepts help clarify how suitable datasets are selected for specific coastal research objectives. Particular attention is given to the spectral behaviour of key coastal components such as mangroves, tidal flats, and other shifting landscape features. Understanding these spectral signatures is essential for reliable mapping, classification, and long-term monitoring. The discussion further addresses advanced techniques, including multispectral and hyperspectral analysis, LiDAR-based elevation mapping, and machine learning methods for image classification, which together improve analytical precision and thematic detail. The chapter also highlights applied dimensions of remote sensing through selected studies, demonstrating its use in ecosystem health assessment, shoreline change analysis, habitat mapping, and coastal resource planning. It concludes by discussing recent technological developments and collaborative efforts that integrate proprietary and open-source tools, reinforcing the expanding capacity of remote sensing to support research and informed decision-making in coastal environments.",
      "abstract_not_available": false,
      "snippet": "This chapter explores the role of remote sensing in advancing the study and management of coastal ecosystems. Coastal regions are highly dynamic and...",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "openAccess_pdf": {},
      "url": "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-032-26828-0_1",
